 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Plane Resolution:** If $N$ is too small, high visual frequencies cannot be represented. If $N$ is too large, plane regions may have insufficient information from LR training images, causing artifacts [S1, unofficial].
-*   **View-Direction Dependency:** Omitting $P_{dir}$ significantly degrades performance, especially for shiny/reflective objects with non-uniform lighting (e.g., PSNR drop from 29.6 dB to 23.7 dB in specific contexts) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **View-Direction Dependency:** Omitting $P_{dir}$ significantly degrades performance, especially for shiny/reflective objects with non-uniform lighting. PSNR drops from ~29.6 dB to ~23.7 dB in reported experiments [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Plane Resolution:** Using $N$ too small prohibits high-frequency representation. Using $N$ too large causes artifacts due to insufficient information in low-resolution training images [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Directional Plane Resolution:** $N_{dir}$ must be smaller than $N$ to avoid overfitting to sparse directional information [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Dominated by the four planes. Total memory is $4 \times N^2 \times C$ (positional) + $N_{dir}^2 \times C$ (directional). For $N=200, N_{dir}=32, C=48$, this is approximately $4 \times 40000 \times 48 + 1024 \times 48 \approx 7.7$ MB, which is significantly smaller than dense 3D voxel grids or large MLP weights for high-resolution scenes.
-*   **Time Complexity:** Query time is dominated by bilinear interpolations and small MLP evaluations (4 layers, 128 channels). This is much faster than large MLPs used in standard NeRF.
-*   **Strengths:** Enables efficient volumetric super-resolution by allowing 2D SR networks (like EDSR) to upsample the feature planes directly. Computationally efficient for novel view synthesis.
-*   **Limitations:** Performance is sensitive to plane resolution $N$. Requires careful tuning of $N$ relative to input image resolution. Less flexible for arbitrary geometric structures compared to voxel-based methods if not properly regularized.
-*   **When to use:** Ideal for scenarios requiring super-resolution of 3D scenes from low-resolution inputs, or when computational efficiency for rendering is critical.
-*   **When not to use:** For scenes with extremely complex, high-frequency geometry that cannot be captured by 2D planes, or when high-resolution training data is available and super-resolution is not needed.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Query time is dominated by bilinear interpolation on four planes and a small MLP inference. This is significantly faster than dense MLP-based NeRFs for high-resolution scenes, as the MLP is shallow (4 layers, 128 channels) and the feature lookup is $O(1)$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is proportional to $4 \times N^2 \times C$. For $N=200, C=48$, this is $4 \times 40000 \times 48 \approx 7.68$ MB, which is very compact compared to dense voxel grids or large MLPs [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** The representation scales well with scene size by increasing $N$, but is limited by the resolution of the training images. It is specifically designed for volumetric super-resolution, where the planes are upsampled using 2D SR techniques [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient training and inference; leverages mature 2D SR algorithms; compact memory footprint; good reconstruction quality for general scenes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Performance depends heavily on the resolution of training images; may struggle with very high-frequency details if $N$ is not sufficiently large; requires careful tuning of $N$ and $N_{dir}$ [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Quadri-Plane Radiance Fields were introduced in "Neural Volume Super-Resolution" (2022) [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on the feature plane representation proposed by Chan et al. for human faces [S1, unofficial], extending it to general scenes by adding a view-direction plane $P_{dir}$ and using coarse/fine decoders. It leverages advances in 2D image super-resolution to improve volumetric rendering quality from low-resolution inputs.
+Quadri-Plane Radiance Fields were introduced in "Neural Volume Super-Resolution" (2022) by [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on the feature plane representation proposed by Chan et al. [9] for human faces and extends it to general scenes by adding a view-direction plane and using coarse/fine decoders inspired by NeRF [32]. It aims to bridge the gap between neural rendering and 2D image super-resolution, allowing for efficient upscaling of 3D scenes from low-resolution inputs.
 
